<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Ilmen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Ilmen is a large shallow lake in Novgorod Oblast in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, drained northwards by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov</a> river towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>. From the 8th century its shores were settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> known as the Ilmen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, and Novgorod grew up at its northern outlet. It lay on the river routes linking the Baltic with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-ilmen.jpg" alt="Lake Ilmen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergei Rubliov, ssr.pp.ru, Ssr 22:27, 3 September 2006 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilmen_Lake.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
